<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mamallapuram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th–8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Mahabalipuram</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mamallapuram, or Mahabalipuram, is a coastal town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, that was a port of the Pallava dynasty and is named after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a>, called Mamalla. Its monuments, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, include the monolithic rathas, cave temples, the great relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/descent-of-the-ganges">Arjuna's Penance</a> and the Shore Temple built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-ii">Narasimhavarman II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mamallapuram.jpg" alt="Mamallapuram" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_collage_of_Mamallapuram_town_Tamil_Nadu_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mamallapuram lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel Coast</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, about 60 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>. Its ancient name was Thirukadalmallai. The earliest mention is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a>, by an unknown Greek navigator, and Ptolemy calls the place Malange. Mariners have known it since at least the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> as the Seven Pagodas, after shrines on the shore, of which only the Shore Temple survives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> burial urn, cairn circles and burial jars of the 1st century BCE have been found nearby. Chinese coins and Roman coins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> from the 4th century CE show that the port was an active hub of trade in the late classical period. The Pallava kings ruled it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, their capital from the 3rd to the 9th century CE, and used the port to launch trade and diplomatic missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><p>The town was named after the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a>, also known as Mamalla, who reigned from 630 to 670 CE and is said to have founded it in the 7th century. A group of royal monuments, many carved from the living rock, was built largely under him and his successor Rajasimhavarman, and they show the shift from rock-cut to structural architecture. The present town plan was established under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a> in 1827.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Group of Monuments at Mahabalipuram is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> with some 40 ancient monuments. They include the ratha temples, monolithic processional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> built between 630 and 668 CE, among them the Pancha Rathas, each carved from a single stone. There are over ten rock-cut cave temples of the 7th century and the giant open-air rock relief known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/descent-of-the-ganges">Descent of the Ganges</a> or Arjuna's Penance, one of the largest in the world. The Shore Temple, of dressed stone and dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, was built about half a century after the rock-cut works.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mamallapuram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade flowed through ports such as <b>Mamallapuram</b> to Sri Lanka, Southeast Asia and China, carried by merchant guilds; a Tamil inscription at Takua Pa in southern Thailand, which mentions a tank named after a title of Nandivarman III and a merchant guild, the Manigramam, attests to these links…</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
